    {
      "id": "xfzt86h",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Himalayan Mountain System"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Himalayan Mountain System represents young fold mountains formed by the collision of the Indian and Eurasian tectonic plates.",
        "The Trans-Himalayan region consists of major northern ranges including Karakoram, Ladakh, Zaskar, and Kailash.",
        "Peninsular mountain ranges include older block mountains like the Vindhyas and Satpuras, along with the residual Aravalli range.",
        "The Western Ghats form a continuous coastal escarpment, whereas the Eastern Ghats are discontinuous and eroded by eastward-flowing rivers.",
        "Mountains in India serve as water towers, climatic barriers, biodiversity hotspots, and critical defense boundaries."
      ],
      "id": "r7muliu"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "72yni5b",
      "content": "The Himalayas are the youngest fold mountains in the world. They extend for about 2400 km from Jammu &amp; Kashmir in the west to Arunachal Pradesh in the east. The Himalayas were formed due to the collision between the Indian Plate and the Eurasian Plate."
    },
